[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Reentrancy Attacks: Single-Function, Cross-Function, and Read-Only"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Reentrancy remains one of the most destructive attack vectors in smart contract development. This tutorial examines the three primary variants of reentrancy vulnerabilities in Web3: Single-Function, Cross-Function, and Read-Only, along with patterns for remediation."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Single-Function Reentrancy"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Single-function reentrancy occurs when an external call is made before state variables are updated within the same function. An attacker exploits this by recursively calling the function back before the initial execution finishes updating balances."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"contract VulnerableBank {
    mapping(address => uint) public balances;

    function withdraw() public {
        uint bal = balances[msg.sender];
        require(bal > 0);

        (bool sent, ) = msg.sender.call{value: bal}("");
        require(sent, "Failed to send Ether");

        balances[msg.sender] = 0;
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Cross-Function Reentrancy"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cross-function reentrancy happens when two different functions share state variables. If function A makes an external call before finalizing its state, an attacker can re-enter via function B, which relies on the temporarily inconsistent state."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"contract CrossFunctionVulnerable {
    mapping(address => uint) public balances;

    function withdraw() public {
        uint bal = balances[msg.sender];
        require(bal > 0);

        (bool sent, ) = msg.sender.call{value: bal}("");
        require(sent);

        balances[msg.sender] = 0;
    }

    function transfer(address to, uint amount) public {
        require(balances[msg.sender] >= amount);
        balances[msg.sender] -= amount;
        balances[to] += amount;
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Read-Only Reentrancy"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Read-only reentrancy occurs during the execution of an external call when view or calculation functions rely on a contract state that is temporarily out of sync. Protocols integrating with this contract might read incorrect pricing or valuation data during the callback."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Occurs when external protocols query view functions mid-state change."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Commonly impacts automated market makers (AMMs), lending pools, and staking derivatives."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Defense Mechanisms and Best Practices"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To secure smart contracts against all forms of reentrancy, developers must implement the Checks-Effects-Interactions pattern and use robust reentrancy guards."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"contract SecureBank {
    bool internal locked;
    mapping(address => uint) public balances;

    modifier nonReentrant() {
        require(!locked, "Reentrant call");
        locked = true;
        _;
        locked = false;
    }

    function withdraw() public nonReentrant {
        uint bal = balances[msg.sender];
        require(bal > 0);

        balances[msg.sender] = 0;

        (bool sent, ) = msg.sender.call{value: bal}("");
        require(sent, "Failed to send Ether");
    }
}"}]
